WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.671
Imagine a new Torrance community facility on opening day.

2
00:00:03.671 --> 00:00:08.437
The building is bright, the equipment works, and residents fill the rooms.

3
00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:11.463
The capital campaign is celebrated as complete.

4
00:00:11.463 --> 00:00:15.199
Yet the most consequential fiscal story is just beginning.

5
00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:17.131
Doors must open every morning.

6
00:00:17.131 --> 00:00:18.419
Staff must be hired.

7
00:00:18.419 --> 00:00:20.093
Systems must be inspected.

8
00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:24.537
Roofs, vehicles, software, and furniture must eventually be replaced.

9
00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:27.113
A city does not merely acquire an asset.

10
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:29.174
It accepts a service obligation.

11
00:00:29.174 --> 00:00:38.546
This episode is educational preparation, not financial, legal, engineering, accounting, labor, insurance, investment, or municipal advisory advice.

12
00:00:38.546 --> 00:00:49.894
Actual operating estimates require current contracts, labor agreements, asset data, service standards, risk information, and analysis from qualified city staff and professionals.

13
00:00:49.894 --> 00:00:52.954
The first discipline is total cost of ownership.

14
00:00:52.954 --> 00:01:08.254
List acquisition, design, construction, commissioning, staffing, utilities, supplies, contracted services, insurance, technology, security, cleaning, inspection, routine maintenance, major repair, replacement, decommissioning, and disposal.

15
00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:16.287
Include internal support from finance, purchasing, human resources, information technology, legal, fleet, and risk management.

16
00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:18.518
Then connect cost to service level.

17
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:24.575
A facility open forty hours per week costs less than one open seven days with evening programs.

18
00:01:24.575 --> 00:01:32.735
A landscaped space maintained to a high presentation standard has different labor and water needs than a low-maintenance design.

19
00:01:32.735 --> 00:01:39.047
Define hours, response times, cleanliness, safety checks, programming, accessibility, and downtime.

20
00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:43.254
Without a service standard, a cost estimate has no stable meaning.

21
00:01:43.254 --> 00:01:45.868
Separate fixed, variable, and step costs.

22
00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:48.928
Fixed costs continue within a range of activity.

23
00:01:48.928 --> 00:01:50.777
Variable costs move with use.

24
00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:57.854
Step costs rise when demand crosses a threshold, such as adding a crew, vehicle, shift, or system license tier.

25
00:01:57.854 --> 00:02:02.253
This helps decision makers understand why cost may not grow smoothly.

26
00:02:02.253 --> 00:02:04.745
Build an asset register before opening.

27
00:02:04.745 --> 00:02:15.291
Each component needs an owner, installation date, useful life, warranty, inspection cycle, preventive maintenance schedule, replacement estimate, and funding source.

28
00:02:15.291 --> 00:02:20.021
Connect the register to the Capital Improvement Plan and operating budget.

29
00:02:20.021 --> 00:02:25.454
A component forgotten in the register is likely to become an emergency request later.

30
00:02:25.454 --> 00:02:27.819
Distinguish maintenance from renewal.

31
00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:32.357
Maintenance preserves function through recurring care and minor repair.

32
00:02:32.357 --> 00:02:36.767
Renewal restores or replaces a component near the end of useful life.

33
00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:42.391
Deferring either can appear to save money while increasing failure risk and future cost.

34
00:02:42.391 --> 00:02:48.783
Track deferred maintenance explicitly rather than allowing it to disappear inside a balanced budget.

35
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:51.403
Inflation affects categories differently.

36
00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:57.731
Labor, energy, insurance, technology, and construction do not necessarily rise at one general rate.

37
00:02:57.731 --> 00:03:00.799
Use category-specific assumptions when material.

38
00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:08.085
Also model contract escalation, new regulatory requirements, cybersecurity needs, and changing climate conditions.

39
00:03:08.085 --> 00:03:11.600
Revenue related to the asset deserves the same realism.

40
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.417
Room rental, admission, concessions, parking, sponsorship, or lease income may offset cost.

41
00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:23.616
Estimate gross revenue, collection cost, vacancy, exemptions, seasonality, and price sensitivity.

42
00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:27.707
Identify whether revenue is legally or contractually restricted.

43
00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.583
Do not present gross receipts as net support.

44
00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:35.377
Use a ten-year operating forecast even if the budget is annual or biennial.

45
00:03:35.377 --> 00:03:41.960
Show the opening ramp, stabilized operation, major repairs, replacement contributions, and funding gap.

46
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.795
Include a downside case with lower revenue and higher costs.

47
00:03:45.795 --> 00:03:52.634
Include a service-reduction case so officials understand what balancing the model would mean for residents.

48
00:03:52.634 --> 00:04:03.275
Consider a hypothetical Torrance mobility hub with sheltered waiting areas, secure bicycle parking, public restrooms, lighting, landscaping, and a small leased kiosk.

49
00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:05.134
The grant funds construction.

50
00:04:05.134 --> 00:04:06.928
Start with operating owners.

51
00:04:06.928 --> 00:04:09.428
Transit may manage passenger functions.

52
00:04:09.428 --> 00:04:12.505
Public works may maintain pavement and drainage.

53
00:04:12.505 --> 00:04:15.197
General services may handle the structure.

54
00:04:15.197 --> 00:04:17.249
Police may respond to incidents.

55
00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:21.479
Information technology may support cameras or networked equipment.

56
00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:24.941
A private tenant may maintain only the kiosk interior.

57
00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:26.479
Now identify interfaces.

58
00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:28.018
Who cleans shared areas?

59
00:04:28.018 --> 00:04:29.236
Who pays utilities?

60
00:04:29.236 --> 00:04:30.838
Who responds after hours?

61
00:04:30.838 --> 00:04:32.761
Who replaces damaged fixtures?

62
00:04:32.761 --> 00:04:35.005
Who checks accessibility equipment?

63
00:04:35.005 --> 00:04:36.736
Who manages tenant default?

64
00:04:36.736 --> 00:04:39.171
Ambiguous interfaces are future costs.

65
00:04:39.171 --> 00:04:41.159
Create three operating designs.

66
00:04:41.159 --> 00:04:43.146
Model one might use city staff.

67
00:04:43.146 --> 00:04:45.902
Model two might contract selected services.

68
00:04:45.902 --> 00:04:50.197
Model three might combine staff, contracts, and tenant obligations.

69
00:04:50.197 --> 00:04:59.107
Compare not only price, but quality control, labor availability, procurement time, escalation, continuity, oversight, and failure response.

70
00:04:59.107 --> 00:05:02.312
Suppose kiosk rent is expected to offset cleaning.

71
00:05:02.312 --> 00:05:04.299
Test vacancy for twelve months.

72
00:05:04.299 --> 00:05:07.697
If cleaning would stop, the funding model is fragile.

73
00:05:07.697 --> 00:05:15.709
A safer structure may fund baseline cleaning from a dependable source and treat rent as support for enhancements or reserves.

74
00:05:15.709 --> 00:05:18.658
Commissioning should include fiscal readiness.

75
00:05:18.658 --> 00:05:28.530
Before acceptance, confirm warranties, manuals, training, spare parts, software subscriptions, maintenance contracts, asset records, and budget authority.

76
00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:34.619
A construction project is not operationally complete merely because the contractor is finished.

77
00:05:34.619 --> 00:05:40.004
After opening, compare forecast and actual results monthly at first, then quarterly.

78
00:05:40.004 --> 00:05:45.709
Track labor hours, utilities, work orders, incidents, downtime, revenue, and user volume.

79
00:05:45.709 --> 00:05:46.863
Explain variances.

80
00:05:46.863 --> 00:05:52.760
Update standards and forecasts rather than preserving assumptions that experience disproved.

81
00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.029
Use condition indicators, not only spending totals.

82
00:05:56.029 --> 00:06:02.504
A department can remain within budget by postponing work, even while the asset becomes less reliable.

83
00:06:02.504 --> 00:06:13.209
Track the share of preventive tasks completed on time, the age of open work orders, repeat failures, emergency repairs, component condition, and service interruptions.

84
00:06:13.209 --> 00:06:16.862
Pair each indicator with a target and an escalation path.

85
00:06:16.862 --> 00:06:18.978
Also examine staffing resilience.

86
00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:23.529
A new asset may depend on one specially trained employee or one vendor.

87
00:06:23.529 --> 00:06:29.362
Document backup coverage, training, succession, parts availability, and emergency contacts.

88
00:06:29.362 --> 00:06:34.042
If a critical capability has no backup, include the cost of creating one.

89
00:06:34.042 --> 00:06:39.234
At each annual budget review, reconcile the operating plan with the capital plan.

90
00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:44.426
An expected replacement should appear in the appropriate forecast before failure.

91
00:06:44.426 --> 00:06:50.836
If funding is deferred, record the reason, revised risk, temporary controls, and next decision date.

92
00:06:50.836 --> 00:06:54.105
Finally, communicate the tradeoff in service terms.

93
00:06:54.105 --> 00:06:58.785
If funding supports only five days of operation instead of seven, say so.

94
00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:03.144
If a lower maintenance level shortens useful life, show that effect.

95
00:07:03.144 --> 00:07:11.926
Residents and decision makers can evaluate an explicit service choice more honestly than an unexplained reduction hidden across accounts.

96
00:07:11.926 --> 00:07:15.653
Your learner artifact is a Ribbon-Cutting Readiness Sheet.

97
00:07:15.653 --> 00:07:19.316
Choose a hypothetical facility or infrastructure project.

98
00:07:19.316 --> 00:07:22.336
Create sections for: Service purpose and level.

99
00:07:22.336 --> 00:07:25.099
Operating owner and supporting departments.

100
00:07:25.099 --> 00:07:26.834
Staffing and contract plan.

101
00:07:26.834 --> 00:07:28.890
Fixed, variable, and step costs.

102
00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.011
Revenue offsets and restrictions.

103
00:07:31.011 --> 00:07:32.682
Asset register completion.

104
00:07:32.682 --> 00:07:35.316
Preventive maintenance and major renewal.

105
00:07:35.316 --> 00:07:36.987
Ten-year funding schedule.

106
00:07:36.987 --> 00:07:38.144
Downside scenario.

107
00:07:38.144 --> 00:07:39.558
Opening prerequisites.

108
00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:41.678
First-year performance dashboard.

109
00:07:41.678 --> 00:07:45.984
Add a red flag beside any line without an owner, source, or budget.

110
00:07:45.984 --> 00:07:51.896
Finish with one recommendation: proceed, proceed with conditions, phase, redesign, or pause.

111
00:07:51.896 --> 00:07:53.695
Now, three recall questions.

112
00:07:53.695 --> 00:07:58.129
Question one: What is the difference between maintenance and renewal?

113
00:07:58.129 --> 00:08:03.141
Answer: Maintenance is recurring care and minor repair that preserve function.

114
00:08:03.141 --> 00:08:08.475
Renewal restores or replaces major components as they reach the end of useful life.

115
00:08:08.475 --> 00:08:12.395
Question two: Why must a cost estimate specify service level?

116
00:08:12.395 --> 00:08:18.756
Answer: Hours, quality, response, programming, safety, and uptime determine the resources required.

117
00:08:18.756 --> 00:08:23.704
Question three: Why is gross facility revenue not the same as budget support?

118
00:08:23.704 --> 00:08:32.122
Answer: Collection costs, vacancy, exemptions, restrictions, seasonality, and operating expenses reduce or limit what is available.

119
00:08:32.122 --> 00:08:43.131
This episode was structured with reference to the public outline of the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy course Foundations of Local Government Finance in the United States.

120
00:08:43.131 --> 00:08:47.776
No account-gated Lincoln lesson was accessed or represented as extracted.

121
00:08:47.776 --> 00:08:53.184
CDIAC's public Elect>Ed outline informed the general focus on long-term consequences.

122
00:08:53.184 --> 00:08:55.857
No account-gated Elect>Ed module was used.

123
00:08:55.857 --> 00:09:11.828
The total-cost, asset-management, expenditure-control, and performance concepts adapt ideas from Municipal Finances: A Handbook for Local Governments, edited by Catherine Farvacque-Vitkovic and Mihaly Kopanyi, World Bank, 2014, licensed CC BY 3.0 IGO.

124
00:09:11.828 --> 00:09:21.691
Changes include an original Torrance mobility-hub example, a ten-year operating lens, service-level specification, and a ribbon-cutting readiness artifact.

125
00:09:21.691 --> 00:09:24.745
The World Bank does not endorse this adaptation.

126
00:09:24.745 --> 00:09:41.607
Additional grounding comes from Institute for Local Government resources on financial policies, capital planning, reserves, and questions for elected officials, and from City of Torrance public operating budgets, CIPs, project dashboards, budget reviews, and ACFRs.

127
00:09:41.607 --> 00:09:44.852
No hypothetical number is presented as a city fact.

128
00:09:44.852 --> 00:09:47.334
The ribbon cutting celebrates delivery.

129
00:09:47.334 --> 00:09:55.224
Stewardship begins when the public expects the asset to work tomorrow, next year, and after its first major component fails.

130
00:09:55.224 --> 00:09:59.960
Next, we compare project alternatives with a consistent decision framework.
